 Let the required monic polynomial of degree 3 be $P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c.$  Since $P(x)$ has remainder $R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 4),$ we have $P(1) = R(1)$ and $P(4) = R(4).$  Similarly, since $P(x)$ has remainder $2R(x)$ when it is divided by $(x - 2)(x - 3),$ we have $P(2) = 2R(2)$ and $P(3) = 2R(3).$  Now, $R(x)$ can be written as $R(x) = dx + e,$ where $d$ and $e$ are constants.  We have the following systems of equations:
\[\begin{cases} P(1) = R(1) \\ P(4) = R(4) \end{cases}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\begin{cases} P(2) = 2R(2) \\ P(3) = 2R(3) \end{cases}
\Rightarrow
\begin{cases} 1 + a + b + c = d + e \\ 64 + 16a + 4b + c = 4d + e \end{cases}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\begin{cases} 8 + 4a + 2b + c = 4d + 2e \\ 27 + 9a + 3b + c = 6d + 2e \end{cases}.\]  Subtracting the first equation in each system from the second equation in the same system, we obtain
\[\begin{cases} 63 + 15a + 3b = 3d \\ 26 + 5a + b = 2d \end{cases}
\Rightarrow
\begin{cases} 15 + 5a + b = d \\ 5 + a + \frac{b}{5} = \frac{2}{3}d \end{cases}
\Rightarrow
\begin{cases} 10 + 4a + b = 0 \\ 15 + 6a + b = 0 \end{cases}
\Rightarrow
\begin{cases} a = -5 \\ b = 10 \end{cases}.\]  Since $P(0) = 5,$ we have $c = 5.$  So the required monic polynomial $P(x)$ is given by $P(x) \equiv x^3 - 5x^2 + 10x + 5 \pmod{(x - 1)(x - 4)(x - 2)(x - 3)}.$  We are asked to find $P(5).$  Since $(5 - 1)(5 - 4)(5 - 2)(5 - 3) = 24,$ we have $P(5) \equiv 5^3 - 5\cdot 5^2 + 10\cdot 5 + 5 \equiv 120 \pmod{24}$ So $P(5) = \boxed{24}.$</think> <answer> $24$</answer>

